# ترجمة 22–29
T = {
"21": {
"en": """It seems to me that the fleeting beauty that shows itself to a person in a quick glance and then vanishes clings more to the soul, and is sweeter. For the image of that glimpse of beauty demands, in the soul, its missing completion, and so the soul falls into a state of yearning for the absent remainder of that beauty, and of searching for it. Do you not see that happiness, which is pure beauty, lasts no longer than the time it takes the lips to part in a smile? Then the soul turns away from smiling and pleasure to its existential business and the worries of living, and the person is left, from then on, in a new and exhausting search for happiness: that beauty of which he has tasted a mere trace, and now covets the abundant flood.

The misery of the miserable, the journey of the traveler, and the migration of the migrant are nothing but one way of exposing oneself to the causes of happiness, and of searching for the sources of more. Ask the migrant, why did you migrate? Ask the wretched, why are you wretched? They are either at the start of their search for happiness, sure that their last steps will halt at the great lake of happiness, or at the end of their journey, content with the honor of having tried, having gained from it nothing but fatigue and toil, though if life called him to its charms he would not hesitate for a moment. Look at the salaried employee, how he spends the flower of his youth toiling, believing his happiness stands some stages ahead of him, measuring it in years and pay raises. When he reaches the end of the line and his strength gives out, he realizes that the happiness he seeks is as far from him as he thought it was at the start of the job, in the flower of youth and the forenoon of life!

Schopenhauer, the philosopher of pessimism, believes “that the happiness of some, and the secret of their success in life, is that they have a pleasant smile; the beholder rejoices at the sight of it and warms to its owner and does for him whatever good he wants!” Perhaps. Who knows? I will try. (The seller of greens and okra in the Nakkasah market, in a timeless smile at sunset.)""",
"notes": ["The Schopenhauer line is quoted as the author gives it."],
},

"22": {
"en": """I return to the file of photographs in which I keep the faces of the laborers I hunt on the roads in the morning, as they drag their heavy steps across the cold earth of exile, whatever the season that covers us. Those who find room in their chests for the intrusion of a stranger who looks at their faces in a way that raises doubts about his sanity, and who even add to that room a kind smile when this meddling stranger confronts them with the lens of his phone or his camera. I return to that atlas of a file in order to travel, for in it, from every distant ravine, there is a face, and in each of these faces a story whose listeners are rare, whose seekers are few, and whose narrator has died. A story of which no speaking witnesses remain but the eyes whose glitter the salt of tears has put out, and those foreheads over which time has walked with its chisel, leaving engravings that seep with fatigue.

I return to that file to listen to them again. Perhaps Uncle Salim has something he wants to tell me about his seven sons. Perhaps Adel has a joke he did not tell me in our first and last meeting, or perhaps some bawdy talk about women that time did not allow Othman the Zol to tell. I return to weave, from the traces of those simple moments I lived with them, fantasies that stop at no limit and end in no time. They take me around their countries and walk me through the alleys of their simple quarters, reassuring me about their condition and their fortunes since our last passing meeting. I return to that file because I want to catch hold of my own motives! I am sure that many of my photographer friends have secrets that make them retreat into the prayer niches of the laboring faces and prostrate themselves before their speaking details. Each has the secret that moves him, and each his story that no one has yet read. In the pictures of some of my friends I read texts that the most eloquent writers could not compose, and I hear in them a transcendent music whose pattern not even a genius like Beethoven could trace.

At a time when the very thought of leaving the comfort of the house for the heat of the scorching noon is a mad idea, these entranced ones go out into the streets and the markets to catch their livelihoods. They do not do it out of luxury. What luxury is there in the scorching heat? What luxury is there in wearing one’s feet down on the roads, on a journey that knows no end, driven by a need that springs from the farthest reaches of the soul and knows no sufficiency? We return to that file because one homeland is not enough.""",
"notes": ["Zol is the Sudanese word for “fellow” or “guy”; the nickname marks Othman as Sudanese."],
},

"23": {
"en": """The tropical-fruit seller in the Nakkasah market, whose name I never had the chance to ask. I had walked past him to photograph his colleague, who appeared with us in an earlier picture, and as I was about to go on, he called out to me and asked me to take a picture of him. I did, amid the astonishment of his colleagues. How could he ask me, a stranger, to photograph him, just like that? He did not know who I was, or what I intended behind the picture. I might even have been an informer, as one of the sellers once hinted to me. Of all the faces in the market, he was the only one who began by asking me for a picture. Simple moments like this are destined for immortality. I wonder how many fleeting pictures have been taken of me whose fate I do not know, and what part of them I was, and what their taker says about them.

(I will return to the same corner of the market in a month, if God wills that the market remain, for it is slated for demolition, to meet some of those whose pictures I took, and show them their photographs, and the good things people say about them.)""",
"notes": [],
},

"24": {
"en": """May you be well every year, and may your Eid return with joys. May God accept from us and from you righteous words and deeds. I have finished the rites of Hajj and the service of the pilgrims of God’s venerable House, hoping that God will accept the Hajj from us and from the pilgrims and bring them back to their countries safe and sound, amen. (Faces of Hajj)""",
"notes": [],
},

"25": {
"en": """The man with the thickest mustache in the camp, and the most easygoing. His friends in the camp teased him and joked about his mustache on the day of Eid, when he wanted to come out of ihram. They told him: “You’re bald, and we think your mustache is nothing but your head’s hair, which has migrated down to your face. Take some off your mustache and be done with it!”

I took this picture in the hardest hour that comes to pilgrims in a hurry, the hour of sunset. For if the sun sets on them in Mina before they have left it for Makkah, they are bound to stay the night, and their plans fall apart. So it happened that the buses that were to carry them to Makkah were late, and the sun leaned toward setting, and tempers flared with fury and eyes with sparks. And our friend here was roaming among the tents looking for “Abu Fahd the coffee man” to brew him a pot of coffee. I went over to him and asked for a picture, and he did nothing more than give this smile, and went on calling out through the tents: “Abu Fahd!”""",
"notes": ["Pilgrims leave the state of ihram by shortening or shaving their hair; the joke is that his bald head’s hair has moved to his mustache."],
},

"26": {
"en": """I have always loved the quiet ones, and if, on top of their good character, they add a fine bearing, that is the peak of beauty and the goal of good manners. This pilgrim is elegant in his ihram and in coming out of it. When he came out of ihram on the day of Eid, he did not forget to give his gray beard its share of grooming and his face its share of the razor, or to adorn his eyes with glasses in a classic frame, and his head with a red turban whose ends he throws behind his head in the manner of truck drivers. When you talk with him, you feel that time stopped in the eighties, when money was money, and Hajj was Hajj, and people were people. Behind this face are the calm of caves and the patience of palm trees. I wish I had sat with him longer, and come to know him better than a hasty snapshot allows. I believe that behind this calm lies a foot that has roamed the fields of life, and a hand that has baked its experiences in the vessel of a lifetime.""",
"notes": [],
},

"27": {
"en": """In old age a man loses the ability to disguise and cover up. The old man will not hesitate to curse you if you do something that deserves cursing, or even something that doesn’t. Nor will he fake, for your sake, a smile that does not spring from what is left of his heart. An old man worn down by the years has nothing to hide and nothing to lose. His long days he has scattered like wheat behind his hunched back, and his short breaths follow one another in a monotonous tune, humoring what is left of the blood in his veins.

The face, in its freshness and the water of youth, becomes a stage for emotions, a display of the tremors of the mind, an exhibition of a person’s strength and weakness. And a man spends years of his life wrestling his nature and fighting the expressions of his face, so that he may live for long years in concealment of his inner world. What, then, now that the freshness of the face has turned to pallor and yellowness, and its smoothness to creases and deep furrows that the hand of time has dug year after year? The eyelids wither and sink far into their sockets, and the eyes bulge as though a prisoner inside those sockets were pushing them out to escape his prison. The face raises its white flag in the face of the end, after blackness, the blackness of the hair, was its flag of rebellion against the myth of the end. The face that was a stage dies now, and its actors wither away, so that the heart may dare, after that, to take the helm and lead the expression without restraint.

The old man has no need to be kind to anyone. The age of begging for hearts is over, and here he stands at the end of the road, having stored up enough experience to curse people in secret and in public. Likewise it is no wonder to find the old man tender to the point of tears over anything at all. There is no need now for strength, for the days have washed away his power, melted his resolve, and returned him to the child who has not yet learned to lie.

The old man in the picture is nearly eighty. He roams the camp like the breeze of dawn, smiling to show a single tooth standing proudly in his mouth. I loved him sincerely because he reminds me of my late grandfather, who would laugh in the middle of his sentence before he had finished it. He savors his own joke before anyone else and laughs at it, and this is a captivating trait in old men. I loved him, and I wish him lasting health and well-being.""",
"notes": [],
},

"28": {
"en": """An Afghan elder. I met him at the Mataf, walking against the stream of those circling the Kaaba, wiping the traces of tears from his face with both hands. For the Afghans, to those who know them, do not show their weeping to anyone, and rocks do not weep, unless longing strikes them with its staff and they gush with springs whose water never runs dry. It was as though this elder had crossed the wide wilderness between the seat of his village, in the heights of the sky, and the sacred valley of Makkah, in order to burst into tears where no one would see him. At the elder’s eyebrows the legions of invaders stood terrified, and in the snows of his beard the plans of the colonizers got lost. You cannot miss the shudder his sharp eyes hurl into your heart when you lift your gaze to them. To raise a camera before those two eyes, which sweat hardness, is a bold ambition. Perhaps a flood of tears I did not witness had broken something of their sharpness and cast over them a touch of gentleness, as it cast over my heart a boldness without which I would not have taken the picture.

This elder reminds me of another, who led prayers in a small mosque in the Masafi quarter of Ajyad in Makkah. The mosque was part of the courtyard of an old house. Its ablution place was a stone basin fringed with moss and lichen, filled by water from a brass tap whose insistence on pumping water into the basin all these years was a wonder. In the courtyard stood an ancient neem tree whose dry fruit covered the floor of the little yard. From it you went down into a rectangular cellar, whose floor was covered by several carpets of different makes and colors, on which the worshipers stood in two rows that were seldom complete. Two lamps took turns lighting it: one white, with nothing of whiteness but the name (if black could give light, it would be the light that lamp gave off), and the other a drowsy yellow that cast its drowsiness over the worshipers.

Under them stood an old man, bent-backed, white in dress and beard, who knew no Arabic but the Qur’an. When he recited the Qur’an you would say: an Arab, were it not for a foreign accent with which he ended the long vowels of the verses. He would end his prayer with the salutation, then turn to the worshipers with a hard, marble face, reciting his remembrances and sinking into a state of praise. And if he saw from one of the worshipers anything contrary to the conduct proper in the presence of God, some chatter or fiddling with a phone, a burning gunpowder would ignite in his chest, and he would shout words of which we understood a little and most escaped us, and none of us dared look at him. He might even stand up and drive one of us out of the mosque, or the cellar in which we prayed. He drove me out more than once when I was a boy, and still I never stopped coming to his place of prayer and praying behind him, for in it I found a peace and warmth I found nowhere else. My grandfather fell ill and moved from his house to ours, and from that day I never visited that cherished cellar, and never knew what became of its keeper. Glory to Him who reminded me of him in the face of this strange Afghan elder.""",
"notes": ["The Mataf is the circular area around the Kaaba where pilgrims circumambulate."],
},
}
